PR #2605  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: scountovf must gate each OF bit on the same mcounteren/hcounteren mediation as hpmcounter reads, or S/VS-mode gains an info-leak side channel to observe overflow events on counters M-mode intended to hide.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_emulate_csr.c:44-149  sbi_emulate_csr_read
      CSR_SCOUNTOVF (0xda0) has no case in the CSR read emulation switch. Every hpmcounter variant (CSR_CYCLE through CSR_HPMCOUNTER31) is gated via hpm_allowed() which checks mcounteren/hcounteren/scounteren per-bit, but scountovf is entirely absent. If hardware traps scountovf reads (e.g. when SSCOFPMF is present but S-mode access traps to M-mode), the default case returns SBI_ENOTSUPP which redirects as illegal instruction — meaning the CSR becomes inaccessible even though the extension is present. If hardware does NOT trap (the normal SSCOFPMF case where scountovf is an S-mode CSR), then reads proceed directly in hardware with ZERO software mediation. In either case, no per-bit counter-enable gating is applied. Overflow bits for counters explicitly hidden from the current privilege mode via mcounteren/hcounteren are unconditionally visible through scountovf, creating a direct side channel.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_emulate_csr.c:20-42  hpm_allowed
      hpm_allowed() correctly implements the per-bit TW (Trusted-World) gating logic: it reads CSR_MCOUNTEREN (and CSR_HCOUNTEREN for virtualized contexts), masks against hpm_num, and additionally checks CSR_SCOUNTEREN for U-mode access. This is exactly the mediation logic that a normative scountovf rule requires — each overflow bit should be visible only if the corresponding counter-enable bit is set. The function itself is correct. The bug is that no caller applies this function to CSR_SCOUNTOVF bits.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_insn.c:48-111  system_opcode_insn
      The CSR trap dispatch chain is correctly structured: instruction decode → sbi_emulate_csr_read() → CSRRW/CSRRS/CSRRC dispatch → optional sbi_emulate_csr_write() → SET_RD → mepc+=4. If CSR_SCOUNTOVF were added as a case to sbi_emulate_csr_read() with proper per-bit gating, this dispatch chain would handle it transparently. The dispatch code itself has no access-control gap — the gap is upstream in sbi_emulate_csr_read's missing case.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:50-61  mstatus_init
      Boot-default counter-enable policy sets CSR_MCOUNTEREN = -1 (all 29 HPM counters visible to S-mode, line 61) and CSR_SCOUNTEREN = 0x002 (only TIME visible to U-mode, line 53). At boot, these defaults are internally consistent: all counters are visible to S-mode, so all scountovf bits being visible is not a leak. However, if S-mode later restricts mcounteren to hide specific counters from U-mode (via scounteren), the scountovf bits remain un-gated. The invariants here are: (a) S-mode must not restrict mcounteren after boot, or (b) if it does, it must also accept the information leak through scountovf. Neither invariant is enforced by this code. The default policy establishes a baseline from which side-channel information can leak when access-control is later tightened.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_pmu.c:338-346  sbi_pmu_ovf_irq
      This M-mode PMU overflow IRQ handler fires for any hardware counter overflow regardless of whether the counter is accessible from the current privilege mode. The handler clears MIE (line 344) and injects an SSE event (line 345). There is no mcounteren check before processing. However, when SSE is registered (pmu_sse_register at line 1176-1182), the interrupt delegation is pulled back from S-mode: csr_clear(CSR_MIDELEG, sbi_pmu_irq_mask()) — so M-mode does see the interrupt. When SSE is NOT registered, the interrupt is delegated directly to S-mode via mideleg, and S-mode can observe PMU overflow IRQ delivery as a side channel for counters it was denied access to. The risk is conditional on whether SSE is active.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_pmu.c:395-430  pmu_ctr_start_hw
      Counter start path clears mcountinhibit (line 418) and enables overflow IRQ via pmu_ctr_enable_irq_hw (line 421) when SSCOFPMF is present. There is no cross-verification that scountovf visibility for this counter is consistent with the current mcounteren state. A counter hidden from some privilege mode via mcounteren could produce overflow events whose bits appear in scountovf. The missing check means scountovf and mcounteren can diverge: mcounteren can hide a counter while scountovf still exposes its overflow bit. This is where counter activity begins, and the policy alignment is not enforced.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:212-230  delegate_traps
      PMU overflow IRQ (MIP_LCOFIP from sbi_pmu_irq_mask(), line 223) is delegated to S-mode via mideleg. Combined with directly-readable scountovf (when hardware doesn't trap), this means the entire overflow signalling path — both the CSR read and the interrupt delivery — can be unmediated by M-mode. However, pmu_sse_register() (sbi_pmu.c:1181) conditionally revokes this delegation via csr_clear(CSR_MIDELEG, ...) when SSE is active. The invariant is: SSE must be registered for M-mode to maintain visibility into the overflow interrupt flow. If SSE is not registered, S-mode receives overflow interrupts directly with no M-mode opportunity to enforce per-bit gating.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_pmu.c:348-382  pmu_ctr_enable_irq_hw
      Per-counter overflow-interrupt re-enable: clears the OF (overflow) bit in the per-counter mhpmevent CSR when MIP indicates the previous overflow has been serviced (line 376-378). This routine does not consult mcounteren before re-enabling overflow reporting. A counter whose events are hidden from a privilege mode via mcounteren could have its overflow reporting silently re-enabled by M-mode when S-mode restarts the counter. The missing check means the overflow reporting pipeline (mhpmevent.OF → scountovf bit → LCOFIP) is re-armed without confirming that the privilege mode initiating the restart should be allowed to see overflow events for this counter.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:130-146  switch_to_next_domain_context
      Domain context switch saves and restores scounteren (line 141-142) but does not save/restore scountovf. When switching between domains with potentially different scounteren policies, overflow state from domain A's PMU counters remains observable by domain B through residual scountovf bits. Specifically: domain A may have an scounteren value that hides certain counters; domain B may have a different scounteren value. After switching from A to B, the scountovf register still holds domain A's overflow bits — domain B can read these bits regardless of whether B's scounteren would hide the corresponding counters. This is a cross-domain information leak that is orthogonal to the primary TW/VTW enforcement gap but represents an additional leak surface.
  [OK] lib/utils/fdt/fdt_pmu.c:37-56  fdt_pmu_fixup
      The DT fixup correctly gates the 'interrupts-extended' property on SSCOFPMF presence (line 52-53): when the extension is absent, the property is deleted to prevent S-mode from attempting overflow interrupt configuration. When SSCOFPMF is present, the property is preserved. This is a static devicetree fixup at boot time — it communicates capability, not runtime access-control policy. The absence of per-counter access-control information in the DT is expected: devicetree is not the appropriate channel for conveying runtime mcounteren-mediated access-control policy. The concern the spec description raises is valid at a design level (S-mode software is told all overflow bits are equally usable), but the DT code itself correctly implements its intended protocol.

