PR #2605  overall: potential_risk
Spec: scountovf must gate each OF bit on the same mcounteren/hcounteren mediation as hpmcounter reads, or S/VS-mode gains an info-leak side channel to observe overflow events on counters M-mode intended to hide.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/encoding.h:1040-1170  (CSR definition block)
      scountovf (CSR 0xDA0) is completely absent from the CSR definition table. No CSR_SCOUNTOVF constant, no DECLARE_CSR(scountovf, ...) entry. Meanwhile mcounteren (line 1075), scounteren (line 1062), and all hpmcounter3-31 CSRs are fully defined. A future scountovf addition here, placed alongside the existing counter infrastructure without corresponding per-bit mcounteren/hcounteren-gating logic in the CSR read path, would create the exact violation pattern: an unconditionally-readable aggregation register exposing overflow bits for counters that mcounteren/hcounteren have explicitly hidden.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:307-377  do_trap_supervisor
      Supervisor trap dispatcher has no counter-overflow interrupt or exception handling. SCAUSE code 13 (LOAD_PAGE_FAULT) is handled for exceptions, but when the SCAUSE_INTR bit is set, bit 13 would represent a supervisor counter-overflow interrupt — which falls into the generic intr_irq_handler path (line 321) with no Sscofpmf-specific dispatch. Any scountovf read added to this handler or its callees (e.g., to service a counter-overflow interrupt) would need per-bit mcounteren gating. No such logic exists. Currently the interrupt would be silently ignored or misrouted, but if scountovf is later wired in, the access-control bypass risk materializes.
  [OK] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:380-449  do_trap_user
      User-mode trap dispatcher correctly delegates scountovf access control to hardware. The RISC-V privileged ISA spec (Volume II, Section 2.1) requires that an attempt to access a CSR without sufficient privilege or when the counter is disabled via scounteren raises an illegal-instruction exception. The existing SCAUSE_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION handler (line 434) catches this and delivers SIGILL via call_trapsignal (line 446). The FPU lazy-enable heuristic (lines 435-444) does not interfere because scountovf reads at user-level without scounteren bits set trap as illegal-instruction before the instruction retires, so the hardware enforces access control. No software-level Sscofpmf emulation is needed unless the kernel wants to implement counter-overflow forwarding to userspace — a separate concern from CSR-access mediation.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_riscv.c:140-159  vmmops_delegate
      Trap delegation from HS-mode to VS-mode only delegates software/timer/external IRQs via hideleg (lines 155-157). The counter-overflow IRQ (bit 13 in mip/mie/sip/sie) is NOT delegated. This means HS-mode always intercepts counter-overflow interrupts. While this is the correct default (no Sscofpmf support in the VMM), it creates a structural gap: if scountovf is later exposed as a guest-visible CSR, the guest would have no direct interrupt delivery path for counter overflow, forcing the VMM to either (a) inject virtual interrupts by reading scountovf itself — at which point the VMM must apply hcounteren-based per-bit filtering before forwarding to the guest, or (b) never forward overflows, silently breaking guest Sscofpmf expectations. Neither path has any per-bit mediation logic today.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_riscv.c:197-238  vmmops_vcpu_init
      Guest scounteren is deliberately initialized to HCOUNTEREN_CY|HCOUNTEREN_TM only (line 216), explicitly hiding all 29 HPM counters (hpmcounter3-31) from guest access. The guest VM enter loop (line 635) reinforces this by writing hcounteren with the same restricted mask. This is a strong, intentional access-control decision. If scountovf were exposed to the guest-visible CSR set without replicating this per-bit masking (i.e., without masking scountovf bits corresponding to counters hpmcounter3-31 whose bits are 0 in hcounteren/scounteren), the guest would observe overflow events for counters it was explicitly denied access to — a textbook semantic-gap information leak through the aggregation register.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_riscv.c:447-524  riscv_handle_world_switch
      VMM world-switch exception dispatch has no scountovf access mediation. SCAUSE_VIRTUAL_INSTRUCTION (line 507) only decodes WFI — any other virtual instruction (including a virtualized scountovf CSR access) falls through to VM_EXITCODE_BOGUS (line 512). SCAUSE_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION (line 489) prints a diagnostic and exits with VM_EXITCODE_BOGUS. If a guest attempts to read scountovf and it traps as virtual-instruction or illegal-instruction, this handler must: (a) decode the CSR number from the trapping instruction, (b) apply hcounteren-based per-bit masking to scountovf, (c) return only the authorized bits. No such logic exists. The guest either crashes (BOGUS) or, worse, if the CSR is silently passed through, gets unfiltered overflow state.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_switch.S:41-204  vmm_switch
      Assembly world-switch saves/restores scounteren (lines 69,75,82 for guest entry; 160,167,173 for guest exit) but does NOT save/restore scountovf. The hcounteren host value is also not explicitly captured at guest entry or restored at exit — the scounteren save/restore pair is the sole counter-access-control state in the world-switch. If scountovf were added to the save/restore sequence without also saving/restoring the associated hcounteren-based per-bit masking state, the following cross-VM leakage scenario arises: Guest A exits with overflow bits latched in scountovf for counters 3-31; Guest B enters, inherits Guest A's scountovf state; Guest B reads scountovf and observes overflow events from Guest A's execution, for counters Guest B was denied via hcounteren.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_riscv.c:161-177  vmmops_vcpu_restore_csrs
      Guest-visible CSR restore path writes vsstatus, vsie, vstvec, vsscratch, vsepc, vscause, vstval, hvip, vsatp (9 CSRs total). The paired save path (vmmops_vcpu_save_csrs, lines 179-195) reads the same 9 CSRs back. No scountovf entry exists in struct hypcsr. If a guest OS writes scountovf to clear overflow bits (standard practice when reprogramming counters in Sscofpmf), the write would land in the hardware scountovf register during guest execution but be silently lost across VM exits because the save/restore pair lacks scountovf. This creates observable incorrectness: the guest would see overflow bits that it previously cleared reappear after a VM exit/entry cycle. More critically, if the VMM later patches this by adding scountovf to the save/restore list without hcounteren-gating, the restore path would reinstate overflow bits the guest was not authorized to see.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/identcpu.c:76-104  (global state: has_sscofpmf / parse_ext_s / update_caps)
      The `has_sscofpmf` boolean (line 79) and SV_SSCOFPMF flag (line 97) are correctly parsed from the ISA string (parse_ext_s at line 180) and populated via UPDATE_CAP (line 467). However, grep across the entire sys/riscv tree confirms zero consumers: no trap handler, no CSR access path, no VMM code, no counter driver references `has_sscofpmf` for any access-control decision. It exists solely as an informational flag exported via md_var.h (line 47). This is per se harmless, but it creates a false sense of security: a future developer adding scountovf would see that `has_sscofpmf` is already plumbed and might gate scountovf-read access on it alone, missing the critical requirement that each bit in scountovf must also be individually gated on the corresponding mcounteren/hcounteren bit.
